Billionaire hedge-fund investor David Tepper's Appaloosa Management was a buyer of technology, energy and bank shares in the fourth quarter, according to a Wednesday regulatory filing. Between the end of the third and fourth quarter, Appaloosa boosted its stake in Bank of America Corp. (BAC), upping his position by 170% to 10.8 million shares. He also increased his stake in Apple Inc. (AAPL), raising that investment to 3.2 million shares and raising his stake in Micron Technology Inc.(MU) to 10.45 million shares, representing his largest holding according to WhaleWisdom.com. The hedge-fund luminary also lifted his position in Facebook Inc. (FB) by about 70% to 2.2 million shares, while adding to his holdings in the tech-heavy PowerShares QQQ Trust Series 1 (QQQ) to 4.3 million shares. Meanwhile, Tepper took a long position in the exchange-traded Financial Select Sector SPDR ETF (XLF), purchasing $280 million worth, though the fund also sold call options in the ETF worth $35 million. Call options give the holder the right but not the obligation to buy an asset a set price and time. Appaloosa also made bullish bets in the energy-centered Energy Select Sector SPDR ETF (XLE) for $35 million. The investor was a buyer in gambling assets, with new stakes in Caesars Entertainment Corp. (CZR), about 5.86 million shares, and MGM Resorts International (MGM), where he picked up 2.3 million shares, from the end of the third quarter. Tepper notably divested his entire stake in Kinder Morgan Inc. (KMI), Chesapeake Energy Corp. (CHK), Whirlpool Corp. (WHR) and General Motors (GM), recent filings showed. Large investors are required to reveal their long equity holdings at the end of each quarter in filings with the Securities and Exchange Commission.
-Mark DeCambre;
